Runtime numeric operations use their selected semantic result type. A failed
check produces implicit built-in failure for the enclosing handler or function
contract. Moth does not wrap the value, saturate it or implicitly panic.

### Checked results

A common arithmetic type must contain every operand, but need not contain
every possible result. Equal narrow operands keep their type, so those
operations can overflow or underflow in that narrow domain:

[table:
    [th: [: Operands] [: Selected result type]]
    [td: [: I8 + I8] [: I8]]
    [td: [: U8 + U8] [: U8]]
    [td: [: F16 + F16] [: F16]]
]

A wider register or scratch value may compute or validate the result. It cannot
expose a wider semantic type, silently wrap or postpone a required check until
assignment.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    overflow I8 = 127 + 1
]

This known `I8` overflow diagnoses at compile time. Runtime operations use the
same selected-width check:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    add_i8 |left I8, right I8| -> I8, Error!:
        return left + right
    ;

    value I8 = add_i8(127, 1) catch then 0
]

Negating a signed minimum also fails in its original narrow type:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    minimum I8 = -128
    negative I8 = -minimum
]

`negative` diagnoses during compilation. A runtime operand uses the same
check:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    negate_i8 |value I8| -> I8, Error!:
        return -value
    ;

    result I8 = negate_i8(-128) catch then 0
]

`U8 + U8` can overflow at `255 + 1`, while `U8 - U8` can underflow at `0 - 1`.
Known values never choose a wider type to avoid either failure. A later cast
or compound write-back forms a separate check.

Failures include:

- fixed-integer overflow or unsigned underflow in the selected result type
- division or remainder by zero
- signed minimum `// -1` overflow
- invalid integer exponents
- a non-finite binary-float result at the selected precision
- a `Dec` operation with a zero divisor or invalid negative exponent
- a compound update whose result cannot convert back to its target type

`% -1` for a signed minimum remains zero and does not inherit the
`// -1` overflow. Integer real division `/` returns `F64` and does not inherit
the narrow integer quotient-overflow rule.

### Binary-float completion

Each binary-float operation finishes at its selected precision. In particular,
`F16` rounds and checks finiteness after every language-level operation.
`F32` may act as an implementation carrier for an `F16` operation, but cannot
replace its rounding and finite check:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    too_large F16 = 65504.0 + 65504.0
]

The constant result exceeds finite `F16` and diagnoses even though `F32` can
hold the intermediate. Runtime code must check the same boundary:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    double_f16 |value F16| -> F16, Error!:
        return value + value
    ;

    result F16 = double_f16(65504.0) catch then 0.0
]

F16 completion also rounds between operations. With concrete `F16` operands,
`(2048 + 1) - 2048` produces `0`, not `1`, because the first addition rounds
to `F16`:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    large F16 = 2048
    one F16 = 1
    constant_round F16 = (large + one) - large
]

A runtime function must finish the same operations at `F16` precision:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    round_f16 |large F16| -> F16, Error!:
        one F16 = 1
        return (large + one) - large
    ;

    runtime_round F16 = round_f16(2048) catch then 0
]

Preserve direct literal and conversion rounding, signed zero and subnormals.
Keep operation-specific numerical contracts rather than adding a new
correctly-rounded Core Math promise.

### Failure delivery

A function with builtin `Error!` as its error slot materialises escaping
implicit failure as `Error`:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    divide |left I32, right I32| -> F64, Error!:
        return left / right
    ;

    ratio = divide(10, 0) catch then 0.0
]

- A private function with no error slot carries implicit failure through the existing same-module failure lane.
- A custom `E!` slot does not automatically convert implicit failure. Recover locally or map it to `E`.
- An exported callable without an error slot must discharge failure that could escape its public contract.
- Entry-selected top-level runtime code runs in `start` with builtin `Error!`.

Numeric operators remain ordinary source expressions. See
@../errors/#error-returns (Error returns) for function contracts.

### Expression recovery

A `catch` protects the complete selected expression, including arithmetic,
receivers, arguments, nested calls and cast operands. Parentheses are optional:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    total = left * right + offset catch then 0
]

Catch eligibility can come from authored arithmetic even when a particular
operation cannot fail. That eligibility does not claim a failure effect or
create an error producer. See @../errors/#catch-and-recovery (Catch and
recovery) for eligibility and handler rules.

Implicit failure and builtin `Error!` may share one handler. A bound error has
type `Error`. A typed `Error` keeps its message, code and identity, including
code `0`. A custom `E!` cannot mix with implicit failure or another typed error.

Catch does not turn a statically invalid operation into runtime recovery. A
known overflow, invalid operator pair, out-of-range literal or other known
numeric failure remains a compile-time diagnostic.

### Compound updates

A compound update checks the revised arithmetic domain, then any required
conversion back. The target changes only after both steps succeed. A catch on
the right-hand expression protects that expression only; later arithmetic and
write-back keep their own failure obligations. Same-type updates have no
artificial widening and narrowing pair. `/=` uses the `F64` real-division
result and the checked conversion back, while `//=` remains integer division.

### Optimisation

A proof can omit a runtime check only when it proves that the particular
fixed-integer operation stays inside its selected result type. The proof uses
complete operand and result domains, not backend carrier widths or known
values. It covers `Add`, `Subtract`, `Multiply` and `Negate` only when the full
input ranges fit the output range. For example, `U8 + I8 -> I16` fits every
operand pair, but `I8 + I8 -> I8` does not.

The proof does not discharge binary-float or `Dec` failure, nor `Divide`,
`IntegerDivide`, `Remainder` or `Power`. A wider carrier never proves semantic
safety. Check elision cannot change source acceptance, side effects or failure
order. It behaves the same across build profiles and backends.

An authored catch remains eligible if a proof removes its failure path, but
that catch creates no runtime error edge. Folding and runtime execution use the
same result types, rounding and failure rules.

### Related behaviour

Range-loop counter updates use the same checked numeric operations. Assertions
remain unrecoverable and checked in release. Allocation and stack exhaustion
and fatal foreign-value integrity guards stay outside `catch`.

Conversions that may lose information belong to
@../casts (Casts). Compound write-back remains the receiving-context exception
described in @./#operators (Operators).
